<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maimonides</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1138–<a href="/years/1204">1204</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maimonides was a Jewish philosopher, legal scholar and physician born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a> in the twelfth century. His family left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> conquest imposed pressure on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians, and he eventually settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His works, including the Guide for the Perplexed, made him one of the most influential thinkers of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maimonides.jpg" alt="Maimonides" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Blaisio Ugolino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Moses_Maimonides_in_Thesaurus_antiquitatum_sacrarum.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…life more than formal statements of belief, though thinkers have defined principles of faith, most famously the thirteen principles of <b>Maimonides</b> in the 12th century, among them the existence and unity of God, the prophecy of Moses, the coming of the Messiah and the resurrection of the dead.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars such as Rashi, in 11th-century France, wrote commentaries on the Bible and Talmud still studied today, and <b>Maimonides</b> wrote his great code of Jewish law, the Mishneh Torah, in clear Hebrew in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Yigdal, Hebrew for &quot;be exalted&quot;, is a Jewish hymn that sets in verse the 13 principles of faith formulated by <b>Maimonides</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/yigdal">Yigdal</a></li></ul></section>
